The Hard Problems We Hit
Problem 1: Testcontainers startup time
Each CI run spent ~45 seconds just starting Docker containers. With 20+ PRs per day, this was significant.

Fix: Layered Docker images. The base Node.js image was pre-built and cached in the CI runner's Docker layer cache. Only the application code layer changed between runs, bringing startup down to ~15 seconds.

Problem 2: The controllable clock was a lie
Our first implementation of the controllable clock in the SLA enforcer simply replaced Date.now() calls. But the SLA timers were actually setTimeout calls in the state machine service, not the SLA enforcer service. The state machine didn't have a controllable clock.

Fix: We moved all SLA timer management into the SLA enforcer service. The state machine emitted a "start SLA timer" event, and the SLA enforcer managed the actual timer. This centralized clock control in one place. It was a small architectural change that only existed in the test configuration path.

Problem 3: k6's JavaScript runtime is not Node.js
We initially tried to share utility code between the k6 scripts and the Node.js test infrastructure. This doesn't work — k6 uses goja, which doesn't support require(), fs, path, or any Node built-ins.

Fix: We extracted shared logic (job type names, rejection reason codes, etc.) into a simple JSON file that both runtimes can read. k6 reads it with open() (k6's built-in file reader). Node reads it with fs.readFileSync().

Problem 4: Flaky invariant: evidence packet immutability
This invariant failed intermittently — about 1 in 20 runs. Investigation showed it wasn't a real bug. The evidence packet was assembled correctly, but the content_hash was computed after a JSON.stringify() that included the _internal.assembledAt timestamp. Two packets for the same task/phase had different timestamps (because they were assembled at different times during the two-phase appeal flow), so different hashes.

Fix: Excluded _internal from the hash computation. The hash should cover the content of the packet, not the metadata about when it was assembled.

Problem 5: Flaky invariant: no orphaned tasks
This failed when the SLA clock advance happened before all message queue consumers had processed their messages. The clock advanced, SLA timers fired, but some tasks were still "in transit" between services via RabbitMQ.

Fix: Added a "drain" step after clock advance — poll the RabbitMQ queue depths until they're all zero, then wait an additional 500ms for in-flight messages to be processed, THEN check invariants.

typescript

// Added to ci-gate.ts

async function drainMessageQueues(env: TestEnvironment): Promise<void> {
  const rabbitmqHost = env.rabbitmq.getHost();
  const rabbitmqPort = env.rabbitmq.getMappedPort(15672); // Management port
  const rabbitmqUser = 'guest';
  const rabbitmqPass = 'guest';
  
  const maxWaitMs = 10000;
  const pollIntervalMs = 200;
  const start = Date.now();
  
  while (Date.now() - start < maxWaitMs) {
    const response = await fetch(
      `http://${rabbitmqUser}:${rabbitmqPass}@${rabbitmqHost}:${rabbitmqPort}/api/queues`,
    );
    const queues = await response.json() as Array<{ name: string; messages: number; messages_unacknowledged: number }>;
    
    const queuePrefix = `test_${process.env.CI_RUN_ID || 'local'}_`;
    const totalMessages = queues
      .filter(q => q.name.startsWith(queuePrefix))
      .reduce((sum, q) => sum + q.messages + q.messages_unacknowledged, 0);
    
    if (totalMessages === 0) {
      // All queues empty — wait a bit more for in-flight processing
      await sleep(500);
      return;
    }
    
    await sleep(pollIntervalMs);
  }
  
  console.warn(`⚠️  Message queue drain timed out after ${maxWaitMs}ms`);
  // Don't fail — just warn. The invariant check will catch actual problems.
}
Problem 6: Signal consistency invariant was too strict initially
We originally required that the signal count match exactly for every job type. But under concurrent load, there's a window between when a task transitions to rejected (which triggers signal emission) and when the signal is actually written to the database (it goes through the message queue). If we check invariants during that window, we see a mismatch.

Fix: We added a "settle" period after all k6 scenarios complete and all SLA timers fire, where we just wait for the message queue to drain (same drain function as above) before checking invariants. We also made the signal consistency check tolerate a delta of ±2 (to account for at-most-once delivery semantics where a signal might be in-flight but not yet committed).

